1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,091
♪♪

2
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,305
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,341
♪♪

4
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:24,275
♪♪

5
00:01:51,645 --> 00:01:53,637
♪♪

6
00:02:21,041 --> 00:02:23,033
♪♪

7
00:02:31,618 --> 00:02:34,497
Now, you're the first
person to see it finished,

8
00:02:34,521 --> 00:02:35,965
so I want you to be honest.

9
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:39,202
Oh. Oh, Amy, it's perfect!

10
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:40,403
Do you really think so?

11
00:02:40,427 --> 00:02:42,972
Oh, it is the prettiest
nursery I've ever seen.

12
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:47,110
Oh... And, uh, Amy
embroidered this.

13
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:48,111
Well, yeah.

14
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:49,575
- Oh!
- It helped the time go faster.

15
00:02:50,804 --> 00:02:53,916
Wow, this will be
one very lucky baby.

16
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:56,052
I'm so happy for both of you.

17
00:02:56,076 --> 00:02:59,355
Go ahead and ask her.

18
00:02:59,379 --> 00:03:00,523
What? Oh, no.

19
00:03:00,547 --> 00:03:01,658
No, you go ahead. Go on.

20
00:03:01,682 --> 00:03:02,593
- What?
- Huh?

21
00:03:02,617 --> 00:03:03,660
Go on.

22
00:03:03,684 --> 00:03:04,894
What?

23
00:03:04,918 --> 00:03:08,131
Howard and I would like it if...

24
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:10,181
Would you be the
baby's godmother?

25
00:03:13,827 --> 00:03:16,773
Was that a yes?

26
00:03:16,797 --> 00:03:17,974
Well, I'm honored.

27
00:03:17,998 --> 00:03:19,642
Yes, yes.

28
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:34,924
Hi, Chris.

29
00:03:34,948 --> 00:03:36,926
Hi. Come on in.

30
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:39,362
Hi.

31
00:03:39,386 --> 00:03:40,630
How do you feel?

32
00:03:41,822 --> 00:03:44,567
Like I'm about to explode.

33
00:03:44,591 --> 00:03:45,935
How do I look?

34
00:03:45,959 --> 00:03:48,171
Tired.

35
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:49,772
Mmm. I brought the vitamins.

36
00:03:49,796 --> 00:03:51,140
Oh, thanks.

37
00:03:51,164 --> 00:03:52,775
Oh, I'm going to need the, um,

38
00:03:52,799 --> 00:03:54,811
final payment for the doctor.

39
00:03:54,835 --> 00:03:57,046
Is there anything else
we can do for you?

40
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:59,382
No, I'm fine. Oh!

41
00:03:59,406 --> 00:04:00,783
What is it?

42
00:04:00,807 --> 00:04:03,920
Oh, nothing. Just
a really hard kick.

43
00:04:03,944 --> 00:04:05,154
I think she's a dancer.

44
00:04:05,178 --> 00:04:07,623
She?

45
00:04:07,647 --> 00:04:11,160
Well, boy or girl, as long
as the baby's healthy.

46
00:04:12,686 --> 00:04:14,964
Here. You need anything
else, give us a call.

47
00:04:14,988 --> 00:04:16,866
I will.

48
00:04:16,890 --> 00:04:18,735
And let us know
what the doctor says.

49
00:04:18,759 --> 00:04:20,236
Of course.

50
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:21,270
- Honey.
- Mmm.

51
00:04:21,294 --> 00:04:22,805
Right, okay.

52
00:04:22,829 --> 00:04:24,240
You take care.

53
00:04:24,264 --> 00:04:25,308
And I'll call you tomorrow.

54
00:04:25,332 --> 00:04:26,509
Thanks, Amy.

55
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:27,592
Okay.

56
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:31,261
Bye-bye.

57
00:04:34,374 --> 00:04:36,486
Want to know how I really feel?

58
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:37,653
Rotten?

59
00:04:37,677 --> 00:04:39,622
Like a dumb machine.

60
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:42,558
Like an incubator hatching
a baby for somebody else.

61
00:04:42,582 --> 00:04:44,727
Will you get me some milk?

62
00:04:44,751 --> 00:04:46,129
Yeah.

63
00:04:46,153 --> 00:04:47,864
Actually, no. Never mind.

64
00:04:47,888 --> 00:04:49,799
- Are you sure?
- I'll get it. Yeah.

65
00:04:51,324 --> 00:04:53,469
Oh! Oh!

66
00:04:53,493 --> 00:04:54,637
Is it kicking again?

67
00:04:54,661 --> 00:04:57,673
No. I'm in labor, Chris.

68
00:04:57,697 --> 00:04:59,175
I'm having the baby.

69
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:01,191
Hang on. Here, here.

70
00:05:10,811 --> 00:05:12,054
She'll be okay.

71
00:05:12,078 --> 00:05:14,924
Jaundice is a very common
occurrence with newborns.

72
00:05:14,948 --> 00:05:16,325
Hmm.

73
00:05:16,349 --> 00:05:19,362
The doctor assured me
that it will clear up in no time.

74
00:05:19,386 --> 00:05:20,463
How long?

75
00:05:20,487 --> 00:05:21,964
Three, four days at the most.

76
00:05:21,988 --> 00:05:24,033
Well, we've waited this long.

77
00:05:24,057 --> 00:05:26,202
What's a few more days, huh?

78
00:05:26,226 --> 00:05:27,570
It's a beautiful baby.

79
00:05:27,594 --> 00:05:29,839
Ah, thank you. I'm Howard Boggs.

80
00:05:29,863 --> 00:05:31,941
This is my wife Amy. Uh...

81
00:05:31,965 --> 00:05:33,476
Uh, we're adopting her.

82
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:34,577
Yes, I know.

83
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:35,711
You are...?

84
00:05:35,735 --> 00:05:37,413
John Franklin. I'm Jill's boss.

85
00:05:37,437 --> 00:05:38,915
How do you do, Mr. Franklin?

86
00:05:38,939 --> 00:05:41,751
Harry Neiman. I'm the
attorney handling the adoption.

87
00:05:41,775 --> 00:05:43,095
- Pleased to meet you.
- Pleasure.

88
00:05:47,781 --> 00:05:51,460
Jill, honey, the
baby is just adorable.

89
00:05:51,484 --> 00:05:52,611
How you feeling?

90
00:05:54,454 --> 00:05:56,753
Empty.

91
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:08,499
Isn't she wonderful?

92
00:06:15,442 --> 00:06:16,986
I need to talk to you.

93
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:18,888
Okay, okay.

94
00:06:18,912 --> 00:06:20,122
Are you okay, honey?

95
00:06:20,146 --> 00:06:22,308
No. I'm not okay.

96
00:06:24,017 --> 00:06:25,628
She's my baby. I'm
not giving her up.

97
00:06:25,652 --> 00:06:27,864
Jill, uh, let's talk
about this later...

98
00:06:27,888 --> 00:06:29,065
What are you talking about?

99
00:06:29,089 --> 00:06:32,134
She's my baby. Not yours.

100
00:06:32,158 --> 00:06:34,904
But you led us on.

101
00:06:34,928 --> 00:06:36,205
You agreed.

102
00:06:36,229 --> 00:06:39,275
Howard, listen,
I've seen this before.

103
00:06:39,299 --> 00:06:41,711
She's just had a baby.
She's very emotional.

104
00:06:41,735 --> 00:06:42,745
She's made up her mind.

105
00:06:42,769 --> 00:06:44,146
Well, I will talk to her.

106
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:45,681
She won't listen to you.

107
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:47,383
Look, let's just calm
down, both of you.

108
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:49,085
Will you stop telling
me to calm down?

109
00:06:49,109 --> 00:06:50,686
You can't do this, damn
it. You made a contract.

110
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:51,921
- You took our money.
- I can...

111
00:06:51,945 --> 00:06:53,789
She's my baby. I can
do whatever I want.

112
00:06:53,813 --> 00:06:54,991
Look, you're upset.

113
00:06:55,015 --> 00:06:56,525
You need some rest. Now, come...

114
00:06:56,549 --> 00:06:58,074
Wait, wait.

115
00:07:00,854 --> 00:07:02,431
You don't know what my wife...

116
00:07:02,455 --> 00:07:05,323
You know, both of
us... Have gone through.

117
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,571
Come on, let's talk.

118
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:10,039
You're not going
to change my mind.

119
00:07:10,063 --> 00:07:12,074
Okay.

120
00:07:12,098 --> 00:07:14,644
Oh, God.

121
00:07:14,668 --> 00:07:16,660
This ca... this
can't be happening.

122
00:07:18,405 --> 00:07:21,671
Oh...

123
00:07:25,111 --> 00:07:26,622
I'm naming the baby Corey,

124
00:07:26,646 --> 00:07:29,258
and I'm taking her home.

125
00:07:29,282 --> 00:07:30,927
I'm not going to
try and talk you

126
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:32,695
into anything you
don't want to do.

127
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:36,632
But I must remind you, you
did sign a declaration of intent.

128
00:07:36,656 --> 00:07:39,869
Don't you think I know that?

129
00:07:39,893 --> 00:07:43,105
When I signed that, I
was two months pregnant.

130
00:07:43,129 --> 00:07:45,474
I wasn't even wearing
maternity clothes yet.

131
00:07:45,498 --> 00:07:47,243
The baby hadn't moved.

132
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:48,644
It just didn't seem real,

133
00:07:48,668 --> 00:07:51,180
but now I've carried
her for nine months.

134
00:07:51,204 --> 00:07:55,151
And I felt her move and kick.

135
00:07:55,175 --> 00:07:57,474
And I've dreamt
about her every night.

136
00:07:58,912 --> 00:08:00,489
Don't you understand?

137
00:08:00,513 --> 00:08:02,091
I <i>can't</i> give up the baby.

138
00:08:02,115 --> 00:08:04,226
Jill, I understand how <i>you</i> feel,

139
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:07,763
but let's look at it
from their point of view.

140
00:08:07,787 --> 00:08:12,335
Mr. and Mrs. Boggs have
invested a lot of... hope

141
00:08:12,359 --> 00:08:14,704
and a lot of money in this.

142
00:08:14,728 --> 00:08:17,960
I'll find some way
to repay them.

143
00:08:18,965 --> 00:08:21,010
I feel sorry for the Boggs.

144
00:08:21,034 --> 00:08:24,213
They're nice people,
but I'm keeping Corey.

145
00:08:24,237 --> 00:08:26,649
I can do that, can't I?

146
00:08:26,673 --> 00:08:28,938
Legally, yes.

147
00:08:31,044 --> 00:08:33,923
But morally...

148
00:08:33,947 --> 00:08:37,426
Morally, I'd like to
see everyone satisfied.

149
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:39,528
The Boggs have been
good to you, haven't they?

150
00:08:39,552 --> 00:08:42,647
Yes, very good.

151
00:08:44,057 --> 00:08:46,535
Then let's all sit
down and talk about it.

152
00:08:46,559 --> 00:08:48,027
Just once.

153
00:08:49,863 --> 00:08:51,841
I'm not saying you have
to change your mind.

154
00:08:51,865 --> 00:08:54,733
It's just the right thing to do.

155
00:08:56,836 --> 00:08:58,361
How 'bout it?

156
00:09:01,875 --> 00:09:04,687
Dr. Addison. Dr. Addison,

157
00:09:04,711 --> 00:09:07,180
please contact Dr. Margolis.

158
00:09:10,717 --> 00:09:13,846
I'm afraid Jill is very
serious about her decision.

159
00:09:16,089 --> 00:09:17,633
Can't you talk to her?

160
00:09:17,657 --> 00:09:19,402
I mean, it's her decision.

161
00:09:19,426 --> 00:09:22,271
Jill is willing to sit
down and discuss this.

162
00:09:22,295 --> 00:09:25,674
But please don't push her.

163
00:09:25,698 --> 00:09:27,643
We won't.

164
00:09:27,667 --> 00:09:31,263
7:00, uh, tomorrow night,
her apartment okay with you?

165
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:35,117
Yeah.

166
00:09:35,141 --> 00:09:37,235
We'll be there.

167
00:09:53,193 --> 00:09:55,438
Now, remember, honey,
we want to stay calm.

168
00:09:55,462 --> 00:09:57,073
Yeah.

169
00:09:57,097 --> 00:09:58,140
How you doing?

170
00:09:58,164 --> 00:10:00,326
It's too soon to tell.

171
00:10:06,406 --> 00:10:07,965
Jill?

172
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:12,502
Jill, are you here?

173
00:10:15,748 --> 00:10:16,943
Jill?

174
00:10:21,888 --> 00:10:23,447
Jill?

175
00:10:43,109 --> 00:10:44,475
There's no pulse.

176
00:10:48,381 --> 00:10:49,658
I think she's dead.

177
00:10:49,682 --> 00:10:51,514
What?

178
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:56,866
We better get out of here.

179
00:10:56,890 --> 00:10:58,234
Why?

180
00:10:58,258 --> 00:10:59,535
Come on. Let's go.

181
00:10:59,559 --> 00:11:01,528
Howard?

182
00:11:10,803 --> 00:11:12,148
<i>Try and be patient.</i>

183
00:11:12,172 --> 00:11:15,484
Ben's probably talking to
the assistant D.A. right now.

184
00:11:18,278 --> 00:11:19,855
What's the point?

185
00:11:19,879 --> 00:11:21,991
Well, the point is to get
him to drop the charges,

186
00:11:22,015 --> 00:11:23,459
so this doesn't go any further.

187
00:11:23,483 --> 00:11:25,384
You think he can
get him to do that?

188
00:11:27,787 --> 00:11:30,366
Ben is surprising.

189
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:33,802
In his own quiet way,
he gets things done.

190
00:11:33,826 --> 00:11:35,604
I don't give a damn, Carl!

191
00:11:35,628 --> 00:11:39,175
Howard and Amy Boggs didn't
kill anybody, and you know that!

192
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:40,209
I have a case.

193
00:11:40,233 --> 00:11:41,877
Oh, you have a case!

194
00:11:41,901 --> 00:11:44,480
You agree that
somebody killed this girl...

195
00:11:44,504 --> 00:11:46,482
Unless she slipped and
fell, which she could've.

196
00:11:46,506 --> 00:11:48,017
We know that somebody

197
00:11:48,041 --> 00:11:49,018
- killed her.
- Killed her.

198
00:11:49,042 --> 00:11:51,153
And why aren't you out
looking for whoever really did it,

199
00:11:51,177 --> 00:11:53,489
instead of trying to
lay it on this nice couple

200
00:11:53,513 --> 00:11:56,325
who only wanted a baby
and couldn't kill anybody?

201
00:11:56,349 --> 00:11:58,494
I'm telling you, Ben, they
had motive, opportunity.

202
00:11:58,518 --> 00:11:59,929
They were found at
the scene of the crime.

203
00:11:59,953 --> 00:12:03,132
I get so sick of hearing that.

204
00:12:03,156 --> 00:12:04,233
Under the circumstances,

205
00:12:04,257 --> 00:12:06,202
I strongly suggest
you consider a deal.

206
00:12:06,226 --> 00:12:09,205
A deal? Oh, come on, Carl!

207
00:12:09,229 --> 00:12:12,374
- The lawyer, Mr. Neiman...
- I know Mr. Neiman.

208
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:14,243
Says that the adoption
agreement provided

209
00:12:14,267 --> 00:12:15,878
that if Jill didn't survive

210
00:12:15,902 --> 00:12:18,247
the baby would go
directly to the Boggses.

211
00:12:18,271 --> 00:12:20,950
Carl, we're lawyers,
too, you know.

212
00:12:20,974 --> 00:12:23,619
That's meant to mean
that they get the baby

213
00:12:23,643 --> 00:12:25,654
if Jill dies in childbirth.

214
00:12:25,678 --> 00:12:28,691
He also said that Jill
didn't sign the paper

215
00:12:28,715 --> 00:12:30,859
withdrawing her
consent for the adoption.

216
00:12:30,883 --> 00:12:33,862
I-I can't believe
that you believe...

217
00:12:33,886 --> 00:12:36,131
I believe what I
have to believe.

218
00:12:36,155 --> 00:12:37,766
Now, here's the deal.

219
00:12:37,790 --> 00:12:40,988
I suggest you listen to
it and bring it to them.

220
00:12:48,368 --> 00:12:50,713
We're in a lot of
trouble, aren't we?

221
00:12:50,737 --> 00:12:54,250
Well... Oh, God...
Honey, it's okay.

222
00:12:54,274 --> 00:12:56,485
I-I... I-I-I-I have
to tell you the deal

223
00:12:56,509 --> 00:12:58,921
the assistant D.A.
wants to make with you.

224
00:12:58,945 --> 00:13:00,389
What sort of deal?

225
00:13:00,413 --> 00:13:03,292
Well, first,
it's-it's an insult.

226
00:13:03,316 --> 00:13:07,029
He says that he
understands that Jill's murder

227
00:13:07,053 --> 00:13:10,899
might have been an act
of-of-of anger or passion,

228
00:13:10,923 --> 00:13:14,303
and he's willing to-to
prosecute only whomever

229
00:13:14,327 --> 00:13:17,706
actually hit her, as
long as the other's...

230
00:13:17,730 --> 00:13:20,909
willing to agree
to what happened.

231
00:13:20,933 --> 00:13:23,579
He wants them to
accuse each other?

232
00:13:23,603 --> 00:13:25,681
- That's an insult!
- I know.

233
00:13:25,705 --> 00:13:27,583
It's an insult you even
discussed such a deal with him!

234
00:13:27,607 --> 00:13:28,984
They really think
we did it, don't they?

235
00:13:29,008 --> 00:13:30,386
- It's gonna be okay.
- I-I-I told him...

236
00:13:30,410 --> 00:13:31,654
- It's an insult!
- Well, I told him,

237
00:13:31,678 --> 00:13:35,224
and we're not gonna
accept his stupid deal.

238
00:13:35,248 --> 00:13:36,892
- It's an insult!
- Yes, it is.

239
00:13:36,916 --> 00:13:38,661
Y-Y-Y-Y-Yes, it... yes, it is,

240
00:13:38,685 --> 00:13:41,030
and-and-and-and we're
gonna get you out of this cage

241
00:13:41,054 --> 00:13:44,099
and into your home and
prove to the assistant D.A.

242
00:13:44,123 --> 00:13:47,202
that he never should have
made his stu... stupid deal.

243
00:13:47,226 --> 00:13:48,270
Okay?

244
00:13:48,294 --> 00:13:50,072
Okay.

245
00:13:50,096 --> 00:13:51,621
Okay.

246
00:13:56,069 --> 00:13:58,013
<i>And so as I got to the door,</i>

247
00:13:58,037 --> 00:13:59,548
<i>the, uh, the Boggs
came running out.</i>

248
00:13:59,572 --> 00:14:02,051
Jill was... laying right there.

249
00:14:02,075 --> 00:14:05,054
So you never saw Howard
or Amy Boggs near the body?

250
00:14:05,078 --> 00:14:06,789
- No, no.
- Hello, Bob.

251
00:14:06,813 --> 00:14:08,824
Oh, Ben. Uh, Michelle
Thomas, Ben Matlock,

252
00:14:08,848 --> 00:14:10,025
this is Harry Neiman.

253
00:14:10,049 --> 00:14:11,060
- Hello.
- How do you do?

254
00:14:11,084 --> 00:14:12,695
Oh, you handled the adoption.

255
00:14:12,719 --> 00:14:13,829
That's right.

256
00:14:13,853 --> 00:14:15,197
Uh, the girl was over here, Ben.

257
00:14:15,221 --> 00:14:17,566
Uh, somebody must
have hit her or pushed her.

258
00:14:17,590 --> 00:14:20,469
She fell backwards and smashed
her head on this marble slab,

259
00:14:20,493 --> 00:14:22,404
fracturing her skull.

260
00:14:22,428 --> 00:14:24,206
Uh, the painter out in the
hall says your clients was

261
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:26,608
the only people he saw
come and go all evening.

262
00:14:26,632 --> 00:14:28,277
Huh.

263
00:14:28,301 --> 00:14:31,080
Huh. Who-Who was
supposed to be at this meeting?

264
00:14:31,104 --> 00:14:33,248
Uh, just Chris Miller,
biological father.

265
00:14:33,272 --> 00:14:34,249
Oh.

266
00:14:34,273 --> 00:14:36,318
Did anybody else know about it?

267
00:14:36,342 --> 00:14:39,855
Yes, John Franklin... he's
the attorney Jill worked for.

268
00:14:39,879 --> 00:14:41,790
Well, did you tell anybody?

269
00:14:41,814 --> 00:14:42,991
No.

270
00:14:43,015 --> 00:14:45,661
Matt Grayson, my golf partner.

271
00:14:45,685 --> 00:14:47,196
Why'd you tell him?

272
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:49,698
Well, he referred Jill to me.

273
00:14:49,722 --> 00:14:51,500
Of course, the
way it's turned out,

274
00:14:51,524 --> 00:14:53,902
I... I may never forgive him.

275
00:14:53,926 --> 00:14:56,739
I understand you were a
little late to the meeting.

276
00:14:56,763 --> 00:14:58,440
Yeah, we got caught in traffic.

277
00:14:58,464 --> 00:15:00,342
- "We"?
- My son and I.

278
00:15:00,366 --> 00:15:02,745
I picked him up from
a Cub Scout meeting

279
00:15:02,769 --> 00:15:04,012
about, uh, 6:30.

280
00:15:04,036 --> 00:15:06,382
He was gonna wait in the
car and do his homework.

281
00:15:06,406 --> 00:15:08,917
You're not thinking, uh...?

282
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:10,886
Oh.

283
00:15:10,910 --> 00:15:12,121
Not particularly.

284
00:15:13,579 --> 00:15:15,124
We're done, Bob.

285
00:15:15,148 --> 00:15:16,992
- Okay.
- Uh...

286
00:15:17,016 --> 00:15:21,130
uh, uh, is either
one of these...

287
00:15:21,154 --> 00:15:23,146
Chris Miller?

288
00:15:29,328 --> 00:15:31,661
You know, Chris isn't
in any of those pictures.

289
00:15:32,698 --> 00:15:34,510
Well, that's peculiar.

290
00:15:34,534 --> 00:15:38,714
You know, I never saw him
so much as hold her hand.

291
00:15:38,738 --> 00:15:40,749
Wasn't much of a love match.

292
00:15:40,773 --> 00:15:44,005
Maybe that's why they were
willing to give up the baby.

293
00:15:45,511 --> 00:15:49,508
Well... keep it up, Bob.

294
00:15:55,488 --> 00:15:56,665
- Oh, thanks...
- Uh, no, thanks.

295
00:15:56,689 --> 00:15:57,966
Okay.

296
00:15:57,990 --> 00:16:01,136
You know, come to think
of it, I never saw them touch.

297
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:03,472
There didn't seem to be
any chemistry between them.

298
00:16:03,496 --> 00:16:05,274
You know, if I
didn't know better,

299
00:16:05,298 --> 00:16:07,276
I'd say they'd never
even slept together.

300
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:08,811
- Uh-huh.
- Hmm.

301
00:16:08,835 --> 00:16:10,546
Did-Did he seem surprised

302
00:16:10,570 --> 00:16:12,915
when she changed her mind
about giving up the baby?

303
00:16:12,939 --> 00:16:14,817
I didn't think so.

304
00:16:14,841 --> 00:16:16,084
Well, maybe they'd
already discussed it,

305
00:16:16,108 --> 00:16:18,020
and he knew what
she was planning to do.

306
00:16:18,044 --> 00:16:20,622
I don't know.

307
00:16:20,646 --> 00:16:24,242
It was as if Jill's decision
had nothing to do with him.

308
00:16:26,285 --> 00:16:27,963
- Hmm.
- Hmm.

309
00:16:58,017 --> 00:17:00,462
Uh, hi. I'm looking
for Chris Miller.

310
00:17:00,486 --> 00:17:02,865
Hey, Chris. There's
a cop here to see you.

311
00:17:05,191 --> 00:17:07,569
Uh, I'm-I'm not a
policeman; I'm Ben Matlock.

312
00:17:07,593 --> 00:17:10,205
I'm Howard and
Amy Boggs' lawyer.

313
00:17:10,229 --> 00:17:12,391
- Come in.
- Thanks.

314
00:17:16,335 --> 00:17:17,335
Hmm.

315
00:17:18,504 --> 00:17:21,149
Oh, you're a musician.

316
00:17:21,173 --> 00:17:22,551
- When I get work.
- Hmm.

317
00:17:23,809 --> 00:17:25,087
Good.

318
00:17:25,111 --> 00:17:27,546
I've been expecting you.

319
00:17:28,915 --> 00:17:30,359
Yeah?

320
00:17:30,383 --> 00:17:32,375
Why is that?

321
00:17:34,987 --> 00:17:37,065
I <i>was</i> living at
Jill's apartment.

322
00:17:37,089 --> 00:17:40,435
I thought you'd be
around asking questions.

323
00:17:40,459 --> 00:17:41,803
Well, yeah.

324
00:17:41,827 --> 00:17:44,172
Uh, did you... did you, uh...

325
00:17:44,196 --> 00:17:47,242
did you find it strange,
or were you surprised,

326
00:17:47,266 --> 00:17:50,178
when Jill decided not
to give up the baby?

327
00:17:50,202 --> 00:17:51,747
No, I saw it coming.

328
00:17:51,771 --> 00:17:52,981
How's that?

329
00:17:53,005 --> 00:17:54,783
For the past couple of months,

330
00:17:54,807 --> 00:17:57,019
the baby was all
she talked about.

331
00:17:57,043 --> 00:18:00,556
What it would look like,
how it would feel to hold it.

332
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,891
I could see she was
becoming very attached.

333
00:18:02,915 --> 00:18:04,893
Uh-huh.

334
00:18:04,917 --> 00:18:07,863
How about you? Were
you... were you becoming...

335
00:18:07,887 --> 00:18:09,865
attached to the baby?

336
00:18:09,889 --> 00:18:12,415
I'm not the baby's father.

337
00:18:14,193 --> 00:18:16,638
I lived with Jill,
but that's all.

338
00:18:16,662 --> 00:18:19,207
- You... you never, uh...?
- No.

339
00:18:19,231 --> 00:18:21,209
Jill was pregnant
when I met her.

340
00:18:21,233 --> 00:18:22,277
When was that?

341
00:18:22,301 --> 00:18:25,347
About six months ago at a party.

342
00:18:25,371 --> 00:18:27,583
She wasn't showing then.

343
00:18:27,607 --> 00:18:29,818
I ran into her about
three months later...

344
00:18:29,842 --> 00:18:32,020
She was showing
pretty good then.

345
00:18:32,044 --> 00:18:35,791
I hadn't had many gigs and
didn't have a place to stay.

346
00:18:35,815 --> 00:18:37,759
She let me stay with her.

347
00:18:37,783 --> 00:18:39,895
I guess she thought
it would look better,

348
00:18:39,919 --> 00:18:41,964
her being big and all.

349
00:18:41,988 --> 00:18:46,668
I ran some errands for her and
fixed things around her place.

350
00:18:46,692 --> 00:18:49,504
I got out of the rain.

351
00:18:49,528 --> 00:18:52,674
I guess I'm what
you call a drifter.

352
00:18:52,698 --> 00:18:55,944
Oh. Oh.

353
00:18:55,968 --> 00:19:00,082
So, um, the meeting that
the Boggses and their lawyer

354
00:19:00,106 --> 00:19:01,984
were gonna have
with Jill that night...

355
00:19:02,008 --> 00:19:03,185
I wasn't there.

356
00:19:03,209 --> 00:19:04,519
- Yeah.
- Look, like I said,

357
00:19:04,543 --> 00:19:06,273
I had nothing to do with it.

358
00:19:07,313 --> 00:19:08,423
Drifter.

359
00:19:08,447 --> 00:19:09,925
Yeah.

360
00:19:11,017 --> 00:19:12,394
Jill took you in.

361
00:19:12,418 --> 00:19:16,231
Uh, didn't you...
care for her a little?

362
00:19:16,255 --> 00:19:18,600
Yeah.

363
00:19:18,624 --> 00:19:22,694
Whoever did this...
I hope you get him.

364
00:19:23,729 --> 00:19:25,140
Yeah.

365
00:19:25,164 --> 00:19:26,325
Yeah.

366
00:19:29,902 --> 00:19:31,513
Mrs. McCardle,
uh, Mrs. McCardle,

367
00:19:31,537 --> 00:19:33,482
uh, I have to go to work.

368
00:19:33,506 --> 00:19:35,250
Now, you know
where everything is?

369
00:19:35,274 --> 00:19:38,353
I do. But you're not going off
without your breakfast, surely?

370
00:19:38,377 --> 00:19:41,223
- Oh, I don't have time.
- Oh, but you must make time.

371
00:19:41,247 --> 00:19:44,359
Did your former housekeeper
let you go off without breakfast?

372
00:19:44,383 --> 00:19:46,294
- Well, I...
- My mother, God rest her soul,

373
00:19:46,318 --> 00:19:49,498
my mother used to say that
you got to have something in you

374
00:19:49,522 --> 00:19:51,500
in the morning to get
the plumbing started.

375
00:19:51,524 --> 00:19:55,120
Now, you come along... I'll
make you some toast and coffee.

376
00:19:56,696 --> 00:19:59,374
I couldn't help overhearing you
on the telephone this morning

377
00:19:59,398 --> 00:20:01,343
talking about adoption.

378
00:20:01,367 --> 00:20:04,146
Uh, yes, I'm handling
an adoption case.

379
00:20:04,170 --> 00:20:07,749
Speaking of adoption,
I've got to tell you this story.

380
00:20:07,773 --> 00:20:11,053
Now, you will appreciate
it, not just as a lawyer

381
00:20:11,077 --> 00:20:12,670
but as a human being.

382
00:20:13,713 --> 00:20:15,924
It happened to a
couple that I knew

383
00:20:15,948 --> 00:20:17,759
back home in Pennsylvania,

384
00:20:17,783 --> 00:20:20,328
Burt and Ethel Steinkemp.

385
00:20:20,352 --> 00:20:22,831
Darlin' people, darlin'.

386
00:20:22,855 --> 00:20:24,666
They owned a
dry-cleaning business,

387
00:20:24,690 --> 00:20:27,785
and they wanted to have
a child, but they couldn't.

388
00:20:28,494 --> 00:20:30,205
They tried desperately
to have a baby,

389
00:20:30,229 --> 00:20:31,807
but it just didn't happen.

390
00:20:31,831 --> 00:20:36,011
They... they hoped, they
prayed, but it didn't happen.

391
00:20:36,035 --> 00:20:40,048
They tried different doctors,
but no one could help them.

392
00:20:40,072 --> 00:20:40,850
Butter.

393
00:20:40,874 --> 00:20:43,351
They were truly desperate.

394
00:20:43,375 --> 00:20:45,420
They tried all the time.

395
00:20:45,444 --> 00:20:47,489
E-Even at lunchtime,

396
00:20:47,513 --> 00:20:50,125
they used to close
up shop and go home.

397
00:20:50,149 --> 00:20:52,027
Still nothing.

398
00:20:52,051 --> 00:20:53,028
Coffee's done.

399
00:20:53,052 --> 00:20:55,297
- Hmm.
- Finally, they gave up

400
00:20:55,321 --> 00:20:56,732
and decided to adopt.

401
00:20:56,756 --> 00:21:01,203
They wanted a child real bad,
so why not adopt one, they said.

402
00:21:01,227 --> 00:21:04,873
So they applied for
one, and they got it.

403
00:21:04,897 --> 00:21:06,408
I take cream with mine.

404
00:21:06,432 --> 00:21:08,343
- Oh.
- And now, what do you think?

405
00:21:08,367 --> 00:21:10,912
No sooner did they adopt a child

406
00:21:10,936 --> 00:21:14,316
than, saints be praised,
Ethel got pregnant!

407
00:21:14,340 --> 00:21:15,584
That's right!

408
00:21:15,608 --> 00:21:18,019
And she got pregnant a
second and a third time.

409
00:21:18,043 --> 00:21:21,189
And all of it because
of an adopted child.

410
00:21:21,213 --> 00:21:23,125
Oh. They renamed him Adam

411
00:21:23,149 --> 00:21:25,427
because he was
the start of it all.

412
00:21:27,052 --> 00:21:29,197
Oh, that's a darlin' story.

413
00:21:29,221 --> 00:21:30,699
Isn't it a darlin' story?

414
00:21:30,723 --> 00:21:32,768
Oh, yes, it is.

415
00:21:32,792 --> 00:21:33,969
I'm done.

416
00:21:33,993 --> 00:21:35,403
Oh. All right.

417
00:21:35,427 --> 00:21:38,306
But don't you ever go
running out of this house

418
00:21:38,330 --> 00:21:40,075
without my getting
you some breakfast.

419
00:21:40,099 --> 00:21:42,277
Oh, no, I won't.

420
00:21:54,380 --> 00:21:56,358
Going in! See you Tuesday!

421
00:21:56,382 --> 00:21:58,326
See you, Matt! Good ride!

422
00:21:58,350 --> 00:22:00,762
- Mr. Grayson.
- Hi.

423
00:22:00,786 --> 00:22:04,099
Hi. I'm Michelle
Thomas. I'm an attorney.

424
00:22:04,123 --> 00:22:05,333
I'm representing the people

425
00:22:05,357 --> 00:22:07,202
accused of
murdering Jill Lambert.

426
00:22:07,226 --> 00:22:09,805
Right. Some mess
that turned out to be.

427
00:22:09,829 --> 00:22:11,039
Yeah. Ugh.

428
00:22:11,063 --> 00:22:12,974
Were you and Jill close?

429
00:22:12,998 --> 00:22:15,143
Well, she was a
friend of a friend.

430
00:22:15,167 --> 00:22:17,345
But you put her in
touch with Harry Neiman,

431
00:22:17,369 --> 00:22:19,381
and he kept you informed
about the adoption.

432
00:22:19,405 --> 00:22:21,316
- That's right.
- He says he told you

433
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:23,018
about the adoption falling apart

434
00:22:23,042 --> 00:22:25,587
and the meeting that was
set up to try and save it.

435
00:22:25,611 --> 00:22:29,057
He called me, Ms. Thomas,
to give me a very bad time

436
00:22:29,081 --> 00:22:30,725
about sending him a flake.

437
00:22:30,749 --> 00:22:33,195
But you knew about the meeting.

438
00:22:33,219 --> 00:22:35,030
Now, wait a minute.

439
00:22:35,054 --> 00:22:37,999
I was out and about
the night Jill was killed.

440
00:22:38,023 --> 00:22:39,100
Doing what?

441
00:22:39,124 --> 00:22:41,603
Would you give me a break here?

442
00:22:41,627 --> 00:22:42,671
Doing what?

443
00:22:42,695 --> 00:22:44,139
Taking an aerobics class

444
00:22:44,163 --> 00:22:47,008
at the Downtown Athletic
Club, if you must know.

445
00:22:47,032 --> 00:22:50,512
I do that three or
four times a week.

446
00:22:50,536 --> 00:22:52,681
Hi, darling.

447
00:22:52,705 --> 00:22:54,449
This is Michelle Thomas.

448
00:22:54,473 --> 00:22:57,552
She's representing the couple
accused of killing Jill Lambert.

449
00:22:57,576 --> 00:22:59,487
My wife, Barbara Dewitt-Grayson.

450
00:22:59,511 --> 00:23:00,755
Ah.

451
00:23:00,779 --> 00:23:02,791
- How do you do?
- Hello.

452
00:23:02,815 --> 00:23:06,394
We're meeting the Parkers
for drinks in ten minutes, darling.

453
00:23:06,418 --> 00:23:08,663
Right. I'll get changed
and I'll meet you up there.

454
00:23:08,687 --> 00:23:10,265
Nice talking to you, Ms. Thomas.

455
00:23:10,289 --> 00:23:11,900
Good luck with the case.

456
00:23:11,924 --> 00:23:13,688
Thank you.

457
00:23:19,265 --> 00:23:22,177
<i>Mm. Is all this stuff original?</i>

458
00:23:22,201 --> 00:23:23,211
Oh, yes.

459
00:23:24,703 --> 00:23:27,249
Daggone! Hmm!

460
00:23:29,642 --> 00:23:33,455
Now, so, uh, Jill
worked for you?

461
00:23:33,479 --> 00:23:36,124
Jill was our receptionist.

462
00:23:36,148 --> 00:23:38,093
I knew her since she was 18.

463
00:23:38,117 --> 00:23:40,279
Watched her grow up.

464
00:23:41,553 --> 00:23:44,132
She was a very fine young lady.

465
00:23:44,156 --> 00:23:46,523
Is that why you visited
her in... in the hospital?

466
00:23:48,427 --> 00:23:49,938
Oh, well, I'm...

467
00:23:49,962 --> 00:23:52,974
I'm afraid there was a
little bit of guilt involved, too.

468
00:23:52,998 --> 00:23:54,509
Guilt?

469
00:23:54,533 --> 00:23:56,811
Well, I was concerned
about how it would look...

470
00:23:56,835 --> 00:24:01,883
having an unwed mother
receiving my clients, so I...

471
00:24:01,907 --> 00:24:05,487
I asked Jill if she would
take an early maternity leave.

472
00:24:05,511 --> 00:24:08,723
That-That scene at
the hospital, I... Yes.

473
00:24:08,747 --> 00:24:10,825
So you-you heard
'em set up the meeting?

474
00:24:10,849 --> 00:24:12,961
I heard them arrange it, yes.

475
00:24:12,985 --> 00:24:14,453
Did you talk to Jill about it?

476
00:24:16,221 --> 00:24:17,866
No.

477
00:24:17,890 --> 00:24:20,302
I left with everybody else.

478
00:24:20,326 --> 00:24:23,405
She didn't ask you for
your advice or anything?

479
00:24:23,429 --> 00:24:25,864
No.

480
00:24:27,366 --> 00:24:28,977
Yes?

481
00:24:29,001 --> 00:24:31,279
Mr. Franklin, Mr. Kerr is here.

482
00:24:31,303 --> 00:24:32,566
Thank you.

483
00:24:33,572 --> 00:24:36,184
I'm afraid my 2:30 has arrived.

484
00:24:36,208 --> 00:24:39,854
I-I hope that I've been able
to be of some help to you.

485
00:24:39,878 --> 00:24:41,389
Oh, yes, yes.

486
00:24:41,413 --> 00:24:44,292
Just-just... just
out-out of curiosity,

487
00:24:44,316 --> 00:24:48,129
if Jill had asked you to, you
know, come to that meeting,

488
00:24:48,153 --> 00:24:50,298
just for, you know, advice,

489
00:24:50,322 --> 00:24:52,834
would you have been
able to, uh, to, uh, make it

490
00:24:52,858 --> 00:24:55,236
or were you, uh, busy?

491
00:24:55,260 --> 00:24:58,206
I would have done
anything to help Jill,

492
00:24:58,230 --> 00:25:00,642
but she didn't ask.

493
00:25:00,666 --> 00:25:02,477
And I was at a charity dinner...

494
00:25:02,501 --> 00:25:05,213
The Autistic
Children's Foundation.

495
00:25:05,237 --> 00:25:06,948
- Oh, that's a good cause.
- Yes.

496
00:25:06,972 --> 00:25:09,751
Yes, a good cause.

497
00:25:09,775 --> 00:25:11,505
Oh, well...

498
00:25:12,544 --> 00:25:15,623
Well... Well, good-bye.

499
00:25:15,647 --> 00:25:16,858
Yes.

500
00:25:16,882 --> 00:25:18,493
If there's anything
else, just call me.

501
00:25:18,517 --> 00:25:19,527
Oh, I will.

502
00:25:19,551 --> 00:25:20,551
Thank you.

503
00:25:40,973 --> 00:25:42,839
Tonight, 7:00.

504
00:26:15,374 --> 00:26:17,385
Hi. What can I get for you?

505
00:26:19,311 --> 00:26:21,280
♪♪

506
00:27:09,528 --> 00:27:11,306
Oh, Mr. Matlock, why
didn't you call me?

507
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:12,874
I would have brought
the paper to you.

508
00:27:12,898 --> 00:27:13,875
Oh, no, that's okay.

509
00:27:13,899 --> 00:27:16,244
Well, you oughtn't to
go out in just your robe.

510
00:27:16,268 --> 00:27:18,213
- I mean, what if I wasn't here?
- Yeah.

511
00:27:18,237 --> 00:27:19,647
The same thing
could happen to you

512
00:27:19,671 --> 00:27:22,183
that happened to a man I
knew back home... Fred Trimble.

513
00:27:22,207 --> 00:27:24,419
He was a darlin' man, darlin'.

514
00:27:24,443 --> 00:27:26,054
Uh, Mrs. McCardle, Mrs...

515
00:27:26,078 --> 00:27:28,623
Mrs. McCardle, I
have my coffee, see?

516
00:27:28,647 --> 00:27:30,758
And so you can just go
about what you were doing.

517
00:27:30,782 --> 00:27:33,394
This Fred Trimble
went out one morning

518
00:27:33,418 --> 00:27:34,762
to get the morning paper,

519
00:27:34,786 --> 00:27:38,032
and discovered that he
had locked himself out.

520
00:27:38,056 --> 00:27:39,567
Oh, well, that happens.

521
00:27:39,591 --> 00:27:41,402
Well, now, the
windows were locked.

522
00:27:41,426 --> 00:27:43,705
His neighbor wasn't at home.

523
00:27:43,729 --> 00:27:46,407
So he decided to go to
the corner filling station

524
00:27:46,431 --> 00:27:48,276
to telephone the locksmith.

525
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:51,946
Just then, a patrol
car was passing,

526
00:27:51,970 --> 00:27:56,651
and they saw Fred in
just his robe and slippers

527
00:27:56,675 --> 00:28:00,488
and immediately felt
that he was an escapee

528
00:28:00,512 --> 00:28:02,190
from a mental institution.

529
00:28:02,214 --> 00:28:03,224
Oh.

530
00:28:03,248 --> 00:28:04,959
They pulled over.

531
00:28:04,983 --> 00:28:06,127
- And...
- Oh, M-M-M...

532
00:28:06,151 --> 00:28:08,796
M-Mrs. McCardle,
th-that won't happen.

533
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:11,466
See, i-if you're not...
If you're not here,

534
00:28:11,490 --> 00:28:14,202
I-I have a key hidden
outside the house.

535
00:28:14,226 --> 00:28:18,640
Oh, but that's really
dangerous, because they'll find it.

536
00:28:18,664 --> 00:28:21,509
It happened to a friend
of mine... Eric Lane.

537
00:28:22,901 --> 00:28:25,880
He thought he had found
the perfect hiding place,

538
00:28:25,904 --> 00:28:27,949
but they found it.

539
00:28:27,973 --> 00:28:30,885
And one night he came
home to an empty house.

540
00:28:30,909 --> 00:28:32,120
Come in.

541
00:28:32,144 --> 00:28:35,823
They even took the Bible
that his mother gave him.

542
00:28:35,847 --> 00:28:36,824
Oh...

543
00:28:36,848 --> 00:28:39,327
Oh, now you have
company, so I'll leave you.

544
00:28:39,351 --> 00:28:40,728
- Uh-huh.
- Oh, remind me

545
00:28:40,752 --> 00:28:44,132
to-to tell you the rest of
the story about Fred Trimble.

546
00:28:44,156 --> 00:28:46,682
It's a darlin' story, darlin'.

547
00:28:51,330 --> 00:28:53,107
New housekeeper.

548
00:28:53,131 --> 00:28:54,108
Oh.

549
00:28:54,132 --> 00:28:55,276
She likes to talk...

550
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:57,011
And talk and talk and talk.

551
00:28:57,035 --> 00:28:58,746
So why are you keeping her?

552
00:28:58,770 --> 00:29:00,715
'Cause she's a good housekeeper.

553
00:29:00,739 --> 00:29:01,584
Oh.

554
00:29:01,608 --> 00:29:05,086
And she's a darlin'
person, darlin'.

555
00:29:20,325 --> 00:29:22,904
Hey, Mr. Franklin, wait up.

556
00:29:22,928 --> 00:29:25,340
Mr. Matlock, what
can I do for you?

557
00:29:25,364 --> 00:29:27,196
I want to show you something.

558
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:31,598
You go a long ways for
a hamburger, don't you?

559
00:29:34,373 --> 00:29:36,384
Make your point, Mr. Matlock.

560
00:29:36,408 --> 00:29:40,989
I think you're the father
of Jill Lambert's baby.

561
00:29:41,013 --> 00:29:43,024
That's why you're
paying off Chris Miller.

562
00:29:43,048 --> 00:29:45,260
She must have
told him about you.

563
00:29:45,284 --> 00:29:48,196
Interesting motive, isn't it?

564
00:29:48,220 --> 00:29:52,066
My God, man, are you suggesting
that I murdered Jill Lambert

565
00:29:52,090 --> 00:29:55,737
to prevent her from naming
me the father of her child?

566
00:29:55,761 --> 00:29:59,407
I'm saying you had reason.

567
00:29:59,431 --> 00:30:02,744
Your biggest client is
the Holy Archdiocese.

568
00:30:02,768 --> 00:30:05,613
How would they feel if they knew

569
00:30:05,637 --> 00:30:07,982
they were being
represented by a married man

570
00:30:08,006 --> 00:30:09,951
who'd fathered an
illegitimate child?

571
00:30:09,975 --> 00:30:13,121
Mr. Matlock, listen
to me carefully.

572
00:30:13,145 --> 00:30:17,025
I am a highly respected
and accomplished attorney.

573
00:30:17,049 --> 00:30:19,294
My reputation is impeccable.

574
00:30:19,318 --> 00:30:23,698
And barring a blood test, to
which I would never submit,

575
00:30:23,722 --> 00:30:25,900
you have nothing to
tie me to Jill Lambert,

576
00:30:25,924 --> 00:30:28,369
except as a friendly employer.

577
00:30:28,393 --> 00:30:30,872
And if you insist on bringing
this nonsense into court,

578
00:30:30,896 --> 00:30:35,109
I'd be delighted to sue you for
an indecent amount of money.

579
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:36,477
Now would you excuse me?

580
00:30:36,501 --> 00:30:39,437
I have an appointment
with His Eminence.

581
00:30:45,310 --> 00:30:48,523
You may call your first witness.

582
00:30:48,547 --> 00:30:52,541
The prosecution calls
Harvey Lang to the stand.

583
00:30:57,389 --> 00:31:00,568
Mr. Lang, you are a
housepainter, is that correct?

584
00:31:00,592 --> 00:31:01,903
Yes, it is.

585
00:31:01,927 --> 00:31:03,838
And on the day that Jill
Lambert was murdered,

586
00:31:03,862 --> 00:31:05,239
where were you working?

587
00:31:05,263 --> 00:31:08,276
I was painting the hallway
right outside her apartment.

588
00:31:08,300 --> 00:31:10,545
Did you take a lunch or
a dinner break that day?

589
00:31:10,569 --> 00:31:12,280
No, I got there at noon.

590
00:31:12,304 --> 00:31:14,082
Worked straight
through till 7:30.

591
00:31:14,106 --> 00:31:15,950
Mr. Lang, during the time

592
00:31:15,974 --> 00:31:17,518
that you were
working in that hallway,

593
00:31:17,542 --> 00:31:20,688
did you see anyone enter or
exit Jill Lambert's apartment?

594
00:31:20,712 --> 00:31:22,724
Yes, I-I did.

595
00:31:22,748 --> 00:31:26,294
Do you see that person or
persons in this courtroom today?

596
00:31:26,318 --> 00:31:29,063
Yes, right there.
The-The defendants.

597
00:31:29,087 --> 00:31:32,200
And did you see
anyone else enter or exit

598
00:31:32,224 --> 00:31:34,369
Jill Lambert's apartment
while you were there?

599
00:31:34,393 --> 00:31:36,738
No, nobody else.

600
00:31:36,762 --> 00:31:38,439
Thank you.

601
00:31:38,463 --> 00:31:40,295
Your witness.

602
00:31:44,069 --> 00:31:47,482
Uh, Mr. Lang, uh...

603
00:31:47,506 --> 00:31:49,817
how long have you
been on your, uh,

604
00:31:49,841 --> 00:31:51,386
uh, present job?

605
00:31:51,410 --> 00:31:52,954
Three months.

606
00:31:52,978 --> 00:31:57,692
And before that, how long
were you on your previous job?

607
00:31:57,716 --> 00:32:00,495
Maybe four months.

608
00:32:00,519 --> 00:32:02,263
And the job before that?

609
00:32:02,287 --> 00:32:05,633
About, uh, two months.

610
00:32:05,657 --> 00:32:09,103
I can, uh, I can have
Mike Foley, Dave Palmer

611
00:32:09,127 --> 00:32:13,141
and Ritchie Morgan come up
here and explain why they fired you,

612
00:32:13,165 --> 00:32:15,543
or would you like to
save us a lot of time

613
00:32:15,567 --> 00:32:16,728
and explain it yourself?

614
00:32:20,238 --> 00:32:22,617
They think maybe I
have a drinking problem.

615
00:32:22,641 --> 00:32:24,952
Hmm, hmm.

616
00:32:24,976 --> 00:32:27,555
Uh, have you been, uh,
drinking on this current job?

617
00:32:27,579 --> 00:32:29,275
- No.
- You're under oath.

618
00:32:31,149 --> 00:32:34,061
Okay, so maybe I
had a beer or two.

619
00:32:34,085 --> 00:32:36,397
Where do you get this beer?

620
00:32:36,421 --> 00:32:39,200
There's a market on the
corner a couple blocks away.

621
00:32:39,224 --> 00:32:40,768
How long you figure it takes you

622
00:32:40,792 --> 00:32:43,805
to go to that market and back?

623
00:32:43,829 --> 00:32:46,924
Back and forth,
ten, 15 minutes tops.

624
00:32:48,366 --> 00:32:51,512
So the evening of
the murder, you made

625
00:32:51,536 --> 00:32:54,404
one of your ten, 15-minute
beer runs, didn't you?

626
00:32:56,007 --> 00:32:57,452
If you say so.

627
00:32:57,476 --> 00:32:59,420
No, sir, if you say so.

628
00:32:59,444 --> 00:33:01,823
Okay, I was there.

629
00:33:01,847 --> 00:33:03,624
Yeah.

630
00:33:03,648 --> 00:33:08,529
You left the hallway, were
gone for ten or 15 minutes.

631
00:33:08,553 --> 00:33:10,798
And during that
ten or 15 minutes,

632
00:33:10,822 --> 00:33:14,302
anybody could have gone
into Jill Lambert's apartment

633
00:33:14,326 --> 00:33:16,904
before the Boggses
got there, couldn't they?

634
00:33:16,928 --> 00:33:18,973
Objection. Compound,
argumentative,

635
00:33:18,997 --> 00:33:20,374
calls for speculation.

636
00:33:20,398 --> 00:33:21,957
Withdraw the question.

637
00:33:24,769 --> 00:33:30,017
Mr. Lang, you couldn't
tell the assistant D.A.

638
00:33:30,041 --> 00:33:33,855
that you left that
hallway and why,

639
00:33:33,879 --> 00:33:37,179
because you were afraid
you'd get fired, isn't that true?

640
00:33:38,250 --> 00:33:39,250
Hmm?

641
00:33:42,854 --> 00:33:44,789
No further questions.

642
00:33:53,498 --> 00:33:55,409
Pending the
outcome of the trial,

643
00:33:55,433 --> 00:33:57,879
Corey's a ward of the state.

644
00:33:58,937 --> 00:34:00,882
But a foster home?

645
00:34:00,906 --> 00:34:03,885
What do you mean "pending
the outcome of the trial"?

646
00:34:03,909 --> 00:34:06,020
Well, if you're not convicted...

647
00:34:06,044 --> 00:34:10,992
No, <i>when</i> you're not
convicted... The baby's yours.

648
00:34:12,551 --> 00:34:14,395
Well, all we always
wanted was a child.

649
00:34:14,419 --> 00:34:17,617
I mean, even this would be
worth it if we could get her.

650
00:34:19,057 --> 00:34:22,370
Did Jill ever say or do anything

651
00:34:22,394 --> 00:34:24,071
to make you suspect she
might change her mind?

652
00:34:24,095 --> 00:34:29,143
Well, she was never really
warm to either of us, but no.

653
00:34:29,167 --> 00:34:30,144
Well...

654
00:34:30,168 --> 00:34:33,681
I never thought she was the
type that could give a child a home.

655
00:34:33,705 --> 00:34:37,251
Well, her place was
always a bit messy.

656
00:34:37,275 --> 00:34:40,321
Well, half the stuff in
it was stolen, honey.

657
00:34:40,345 --> 00:34:41,322
Stolen?

658
00:34:41,346 --> 00:34:43,724
Not half. There was some soap

659
00:34:43,748 --> 00:34:45,393
and a shopping cart in the hall

660
00:34:45,417 --> 00:34:47,061
and some towels,
that sort of thing.

661
00:34:47,085 --> 00:34:48,362
She stole soap?

662
00:34:48,386 --> 00:34:50,998
Yes, um, I think it was from

663
00:34:51,022 --> 00:34:54,535
that little French hotel
downtown... the Chåteau de...

664
00:34:54,559 --> 00:34:55,670
Chåteau de Fleurs?

665
00:34:55,694 --> 00:34:57,560
Yes, I think that
was it, mm-hmm.

666
00:34:58,663 --> 00:34:59,663
Hmm.

667
00:35:09,608 --> 00:35:11,919
<i>- Bonsoir, madame.
- Bonsoir.</i>

668
00:35:11,943 --> 00:35:13,888
I was wondering if
you could help me.

669
00:35:13,912 --> 00:35:16,991
<i>Avec plaisir, madame.
Certainement.</i>

670
00:35:17,015 --> 00:35:20,884
Do you, uh, do you
know this woman?

671
00:35:22,087 --> 00:35:24,750
<i>Mais oui. Oui, je la connais.</i>

672
00:35:25,890 --> 00:35:28,903
She has been a
guest here many times.

673
00:35:28,927 --> 00:35:30,438
Though not for a little while.

674
00:35:30,462 --> 00:35:33,364
Perhaps, uh, seven
or eight months.

675
00:35:35,600 --> 00:35:37,678
Would she come in with a man?

676
00:35:37,702 --> 00:35:40,348
Always.

677
00:35:40,372 --> 00:35:42,116
Is this him?

678
00:35:42,140 --> 00:35:44,752
No. No, no.

679
00:35:44,776 --> 00:35:46,187
He was younger.

680
00:35:46,211 --> 00:35:47,655
Do you remember his name?

681
00:35:47,679 --> 00:35:51,759
Monsieur Smith.

682
00:35:51,783 --> 00:35:54,261
And he always paid cash.

683
00:35:54,285 --> 00:35:56,345
<i>Merci.</i>

684
00:36:10,635 --> 00:36:13,481
No, Mr. Grayson didn't attend
his aerobics class that day.

685
00:36:13,505 --> 00:36:15,249
How can you be so sure?

686
00:36:15,273 --> 00:36:16,917
All the classes that
day were canceled.

687
00:36:16,941 --> 00:36:19,220
Air conditioning went out.

688
00:36:19,244 --> 00:36:20,888
Are you sure it was that day?

689
00:36:20,912 --> 00:36:22,823
Believe me, I remember
the complaining.

690
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:24,179
You'd think I broke it myself.

691
00:36:44,069 --> 00:36:45,435
Matt Grayson?

692
00:36:46,571 --> 00:36:47,548
Do I know you?

693
00:36:47,572 --> 00:36:48,983
No.

694
00:36:49,007 --> 00:36:51,485
Now, what's this garbage
you're spouting on the phone?

695
00:36:51,509 --> 00:36:53,154
If it was garbage,
you wouldn't be here.

696
00:36:53,178 --> 00:36:54,178
Have a seat.

697
00:37:03,354 --> 00:37:04,498
What do you want?

698
00:37:04,522 --> 00:37:06,934
What would your
wife think if she knew

699
00:37:06,958 --> 00:37:08,969
you were the father
of Jill Lambert's baby?

700
00:37:08,993 --> 00:37:10,538
That's a lie.

701
00:37:10,562 --> 00:37:11,539
Is it?

702
00:37:11,563 --> 00:37:12,807
I think you and I know better.

703
00:37:12,831 --> 00:37:14,608
And I think you're gonna
come up with $20,000

704
00:37:14,632 --> 00:37:15,632
to keep it quiet.

705
00:37:16,768 --> 00:37:19,847
Look, boy, you're
out of your league.

706
00:37:21,206 --> 00:37:22,650
Okay.

707
00:37:22,674 --> 00:37:24,518
I'll see you in the
<i>National Informer.</i>

708
00:37:24,542 --> 00:37:26,443
Wait.

709
00:37:27,545 --> 00:37:29,356
I can't get that kind of money.

710
00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:31,258
Yes, you can.

711
00:37:31,282 --> 00:37:34,361
All right, all
right, I'll get it.

712
00:37:34,385 --> 00:37:36,130
Fine.

713
00:37:36,154 --> 00:37:37,986
Smart move.

714
00:37:39,991 --> 00:37:42,403
How do I know
this'll be the end?

715
00:37:42,427 --> 00:37:44,305
You don't.

716
00:37:44,329 --> 00:37:47,299
6:00 tomorrow, Dave's
burger stand on Peach Street.

717
00:38:02,547 --> 00:38:04,209
You could get me
killed doing this.

718
00:38:18,997 --> 00:38:21,342
So which one of them
do <i>you</i> think is the father?

719
00:38:21,366 --> 00:38:22,743
Oh, I don't know.

720
00:38:22,767 --> 00:38:26,647
John Franklin and Matt
Grayson both think they are.

721
00:38:26,671 --> 00:38:28,315
And they both have a lot to lose

722
00:38:28,339 --> 00:38:31,252
if Chris starts making
accusations in public.

723
00:38:31,276 --> 00:38:33,721
Well, one of them must
have been here that night.

724
00:38:33,745 --> 00:38:35,556
Yeah.

725
00:38:35,580 --> 00:38:37,158
Oh.

726
00:38:37,182 --> 00:38:39,160
Brand-new baby blanket.

727
00:38:39,184 --> 00:38:41,228
She didn't even have
time to take the tags off.

728
00:38:41,252 --> 00:38:42,252
Yeah.

729
00:38:46,558 --> 00:38:50,171
Baby Corey's a girl, isn't she?

730
00:38:50,195 --> 00:38:52,187
Yeah.

731
00:39:00,104 --> 00:39:02,539
<i>Well, now, Mr. Grayson...</i>

732
00:39:03,741 --> 00:39:08,222
What was your relationship
with the deceased?

733
00:39:08,246 --> 00:39:09,657
No relationship, really.

734
00:39:09,681 --> 00:39:12,960
A friend introduced
us at a party.

735
00:39:12,984 --> 00:39:17,264
Miss Lambert was distressed
and very open about the reason.

736
00:39:17,288 --> 00:39:19,533
She said she was
pregnant out of wedlock

737
00:39:19,557 --> 00:39:23,370
and had decided it was best
to give the baby up for adoption.

738
00:39:23,394 --> 00:39:25,039
I referred her to
an attorney friend

739
00:39:25,063 --> 00:39:26,173
who specializes in adoption.

740
00:39:26,197 --> 00:39:27,174
Harry Neiman.

741
00:39:27,198 --> 00:39:29,577
- That's right.
- And so, uh,

742
00:39:29,601 --> 00:39:32,880
after that party, you never
saw Jill Lambert again?

743
00:39:32,904 --> 00:39:33,881
No.

744
00:39:33,905 --> 00:39:35,216
Oh.

745
00:39:35,240 --> 00:39:37,318
Uh, so, uh, um, uh...

746
00:39:37,342 --> 00:39:39,987
what business are
you in, Mr. Grayson?

747
00:39:40,011 --> 00:39:41,522
I'm a real estate developer.

748
00:39:41,546 --> 00:39:45,025
Real estate's in a kind of
a slump right now, isn't it?

749
00:39:45,049 --> 00:39:46,694
Well, that depends
on the real estate.

750
00:39:46,718 --> 00:39:48,696
Yeah. Well, of course.

751
00:39:48,720 --> 00:39:50,064
See, you still need a lot of,

752
00:39:50,088 --> 00:39:52,066
lot of money, a
lot of cash up front.

753
00:39:52,090 --> 00:39:53,667
Do you have-have partners?

754
00:39:53,691 --> 00:39:55,936
Uh, not exactly.

755
00:39:55,960 --> 00:39:56,905
Is that a yes or no?

756
00:39:56,929 --> 00:39:58,739
My wife and I are partners.

757
00:39:58,763 --> 00:40:00,040
Your Honor,

758
00:40:00,064 --> 00:40:02,910
this is all very interesting
but hardly relevant.

759
00:40:02,934 --> 00:40:05,479
Uh, Your Honor,

760
00:40:05,503 --> 00:40:08,048
if you'll bear with
me, I'll show you

761
00:40:08,072 --> 00:40:11,018
that this direction is
much more than relevant.

762
00:40:11,042 --> 00:40:12,920
Overruled.

763
00:40:12,944 --> 00:40:15,890
Continue, Mr. Matlock,
but get to the point.

764
00:40:15,914 --> 00:40:17,191
Yes, sir.

765
00:40:17,215 --> 00:40:19,760
Must be interesting having
your wife as a partner.

766
00:40:19,784 --> 00:40:22,763
Uh, is everything split
50-50 right down the middle?

767
00:40:22,787 --> 00:40:24,064
Not exactly.

768
00:40:24,088 --> 00:40:26,200
Oh, you mean
it's-it's your business

769
00:40:26,224 --> 00:40:27,735
and you take the larger share?

770
00:40:27,759 --> 00:40:30,404
I don't take a larger share.

771
00:40:30,428 --> 00:40:32,273
And it's my wife's company.

772
00:40:32,297 --> 00:40:33,774
Uh-huh.

773
00:40:33,798 --> 00:40:37,077
Your wife is Barbara
Dewitt-Grayson, the heiress.

774
00:40:37,101 --> 00:40:39,313
That's right.

775
00:40:39,337 --> 00:40:40,414
Uh, at the time

776
00:40:40,438 --> 00:40:44,952
Jill Lambert was, uh,
murdered, you were taking

777
00:40:44,976 --> 00:40:48,322
an aerobics class at the,
uh, Downtown Athletic Club.

778
00:40:48,346 --> 00:40:49,323
- Correct?
- That's right.

779
00:40:49,347 --> 00:40:51,692
- Yeah.
- As I told your associate.

780
00:40:51,716 --> 00:40:54,094
Yeah, but that-that
wasn't quite true, was it?

781
00:40:54,118 --> 00:40:57,965
Because, uh, that day, the
air conditioning broke down

782
00:40:57,989 --> 00:40:59,800
and they canceled all classes.

783
00:40:59,824 --> 00:41:02,002
So you couldn't have been
there that day, could you?

784
00:41:02,026 --> 00:41:03,671
Maybe I got my days mixed up.

785
00:41:03,695 --> 00:41:04,838
Yeah, maybe you did.

786
00:41:04,862 --> 00:41:06,206
Maybe you did.

787
00:41:06,230 --> 00:41:08,409
Uh, do you recognize this man?

788
00:41:08,433 --> 00:41:12,295
Yeah... Do you...?

789
00:41:14,005 --> 00:41:15,349
I don't know him, no.

790
00:41:15,373 --> 00:41:18,419
If I told you that
he is the desk clerk

791
00:41:18,443 --> 00:41:22,756
at the Chåteau de
Fleurs Hotel downtown,

792
00:41:22,780 --> 00:41:24,224
would you recognize him then?

793
00:41:24,248 --> 00:41:26,160
No.

794
00:41:26,184 --> 00:41:29,396
Well, he's prepared
to testify that you were

795
00:41:29,420 --> 00:41:33,400
a frequent guest
for a lengthy interval

796
00:41:33,424 --> 00:41:34,668
at his hotel.

797
00:41:34,692 --> 00:41:37,771
Only you used the name Smith.

798
00:41:37,795 --> 00:41:40,641
Who was your companion
during those stays?

799
00:41:40,665 --> 00:41:42,810
It was Jill Lambert, wasn't it?

800
00:41:42,834 --> 00:41:45,235
He's prepared to testify.

801
00:41:46,838 --> 00:41:49,364
So were you having an
affair with Jill Lambert?

802
00:41:52,410 --> 00:41:53,787
Yes, I was.

803
00:41:53,811 --> 00:41:56,056
Affairs outside of
marriage can cause

804
00:41:56,080 --> 00:41:58,826
a world of trouble inside
the marriage, can't they?

805
00:41:58,850 --> 00:42:00,627
My marriage to Barbara is solid,

806
00:42:00,651 --> 00:42:02,596
if it's any of your business.

807
00:42:02,620 --> 00:42:05,332
Oh, well... would it have, uh,

808
00:42:05,356 --> 00:42:09,203
remained solid when the
word came out that you were

809
00:42:09,227 --> 00:42:11,038
the father of Jill
Lambert's baby?

810
00:42:11,062 --> 00:42:12,606
You don't know I was the father.

811
00:42:12,630 --> 00:42:13,807
No, I don't.

812
00:42:13,831 --> 00:42:16,844
But you had good reason
to believe you were.

813
00:42:16,868 --> 00:42:20,848
And when she changed her mind
and decided to keep that baby,

814
00:42:20,872 --> 00:42:23,083
what'd she ask you,
for child support?

815
00:42:23,107 --> 00:42:24,651
Doesn't mean I'd kill her.

816
00:42:24,675 --> 00:42:27,187
It still made for a
sticky situation, didn't it?

817
00:42:27,211 --> 00:42:29,356
I mean, how would you
explain that money away

818
00:42:29,380 --> 00:42:31,558
to your heiress wife?

819
00:42:32,817 --> 00:42:34,828
When-When Jill
was prepared to do

820
00:42:34,852 --> 00:42:38,632
the right thing for you
and give up the baby,

821
00:42:38,656 --> 00:42:40,000
you were happy.

822
00:42:40,024 --> 00:42:43,303
But when she decided to
keep it, you were in trouble.

823
00:42:43,327 --> 00:42:45,372
No, I wasn't.

824
00:42:45,396 --> 00:42:47,041
Didn't your friend
Harry Neiman call you

825
00:42:47,065 --> 00:42:50,744
and tell you about the
meeting at her apartment

826
00:42:50,768 --> 00:42:52,703
that evening at 7:00
with the Boggses?

827
00:42:54,038 --> 00:42:55,616
- I suppose.
- Yeah.

828
00:42:55,640 --> 00:42:59,086
The only choice you had
was to get to her apartment

829
00:42:59,110 --> 00:43:02,356
before they did and
try to convince her

830
00:43:02,380 --> 00:43:04,625
to go ahead with the adoption.

831
00:43:04,649 --> 00:43:06,727
So you went to her apartment.

832
00:43:06,751 --> 00:43:08,896
By now, she really
wanted to keep that baby.

833
00:43:08,920 --> 00:43:10,431
She wouldn't listen to you.

834
00:43:10,455 --> 00:43:11,799
You pled with her,
she wouldn't listen.

835
00:43:11,823 --> 00:43:13,233
You argued with her,
she wouldn't listen.

836
00:43:13,257 --> 00:43:14,968
You got angry.
You-You pushed her

837
00:43:14,992 --> 00:43:18,472
or you hit her, and she fell
on the apron of that fireplace

838
00:43:18,496 --> 00:43:19,706
and fractured her skull,

839
00:43:19,730 --> 00:43:21,375
died just like that!

840
00:43:21,399 --> 00:43:24,878
And you did the only thing any
red-blooded coward could do;

841
00:43:24,902 --> 00:43:27,414
you ran and left the
Boggses to take the blame.

842
00:43:27,438 --> 00:43:28,415
You're wrong!

843
00:43:28,439 --> 00:43:29,750
I wasn't even there.

844
00:43:29,774 --> 00:43:31,718
- Are you sure?
- Yeah, I'm sure.

845
00:43:31,742 --> 00:43:33,420
Do you recognize this?

846
00:43:33,444 --> 00:43:35,055
No, I don't!

847
00:43:35,079 --> 00:43:39,744
I found this in Jill
Lambert's apartment.

848
00:43:41,018 --> 00:43:44,364
The name of the
store is on the tag.

849
00:43:44,388 --> 00:43:48,435
And their records show
that this was bought

850
00:43:48,459 --> 00:43:51,505
an hour before Jill
Lambert was murdered.

851
00:43:51,529 --> 00:43:55,242
You bought this
blanket and took it to her

852
00:43:55,266 --> 00:44:01,115
as a peace offering maybe,
to show that you were sincere

853
00:44:01,139 --> 00:44:04,151
and to convince her to
go ahead with the adoption.

854
00:44:04,175 --> 00:44:05,486
Correct?

855
00:44:05,510 --> 00:44:08,689
No. Your Honor, this
man's making all this up.

856
00:44:08,713 --> 00:44:10,757
Almost done, Your
Honor, almost done!

857
00:44:10,781 --> 00:44:12,793
Proceed.

858
00:44:12,817 --> 00:44:17,598
Everybody who knew
about that meeting

859
00:44:17,622 --> 00:44:20,717
was at the hospital but you.

860
00:44:21,759 --> 00:44:25,572
Everybody at that hospital knew

861
00:44:25,596 --> 00:44:28,709
that baby was a
girl named Corey,

862
00:44:28,733 --> 00:44:31,411
except you.

863
00:44:31,435 --> 00:44:33,847
Only somebody who
wasn't at that hospital

864
00:44:33,871 --> 00:44:38,218
would think Corey
was a boy's name

865
00:44:38,242 --> 00:44:41,255
and buy a blue blanket.

866
00:44:41,279 --> 00:44:46,059
You bought this blanket,
and you murdered Jill Lambert.

867
00:44:46,083 --> 00:44:47,779
That's a lie.

868
00:44:49,820 --> 00:44:53,222
And here is the
receipt for the blanket.

869
00:44:54,625 --> 00:44:57,437
Purchased on your credit card.

870
00:44:57,461 --> 00:44:59,473
Your Honor.

871
00:44:59,497 --> 00:45:01,489
See there, Carl?

872
00:45:04,669 --> 00:45:08,015
Reasonable doubt,
ladies and gentlemen.

873
00:45:08,039 --> 00:45:10,884
Can any of you say

874
00:45:10,908 --> 00:45:13,921
beyond a reasonable
doubt that my clients

875
00:45:13,945 --> 00:45:16,256
murdered Jill Lambert?

876
00:45:16,280 --> 00:45:18,225
I don't think so.

877
00:45:18,249 --> 00:45:21,828
And when the Atlanta Police
finish their investigation,

878
00:45:21,852 --> 00:45:26,633
I think we'll find the person
who killed Jill Lambert

879
00:45:26,657 --> 00:45:28,125
is sitting right there.

880
00:45:38,169 --> 00:45:40,080
I can't tell you
how happy we are.

881
00:45:40,104 --> 00:45:41,882
And how wonderful
you've been, both of you.

882
00:45:41,906 --> 00:45:43,517
Well, I'm so glad we could help.

883
00:45:43,541 --> 00:45:46,420
I, uh, I told Ben I was
gonna take us all out to lunch.

884
00:45:46,444 --> 00:45:47,421
Where is he?

885
00:45:47,445 --> 00:45:48,989
He's running an errand.

886
00:45:49,013 --> 00:45:51,124
He said he has
something to pick up.

887
00:45:57,421 --> 00:45:58,932
We can... we can have her?

888
00:45:58,956 --> 00:45:59,933
Right now?

889
00:45:59,957 --> 00:46:00,934
Absolutely.

890
00:46:00,958 --> 00:46:02,169
How can we ever thank you?

891
00:46:02,193 --> 00:46:03,971
Well, why don't you just
go ahead and take her?

892
00:46:03,995 --> 00:46:05,105
She's a little wet.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
